# Navigation and cross-country planning: PHAK chapter 16; AIM chapters 1, 4, 5 and 6; 14 CFR 91.151, 91.159.
# ACS I.D, VI.A to VI.D. Wind triangles solved with the sine rule; all numbers in the stem.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 3, "Wind correction", "True course is 090, true airspeed is 120 knots and the wind is from 360 degrees at 20 knots. About what are the true heading and groundspeed?",
      ["080 degrees and 118 knots", "100 degrees and 118 knots", "080 degrees and 120 knots"],
      "The wind is directly off the left side. Wind correction angle = arcsin(20/120), about 9.6 degrees left, so heading is about 080. Groundspeed = square root of (120 squared minus 20 squared), about 118 knots."),
    S(1, 2, "Fuel planning", "A day VFR leg is 280 nm at a groundspeed of 140 knots and the airplane burns 12 gallons per hour. Ignoring taxi and climb, what is the minimum fuel to depart with?",
      ["30 gallons", "24 gallons", "33 gallons"],
      "Flight time is 280 / 140 = 2.0 hours, or 24 gallons. Day VFR needs enough fuel to reach the destination plus 30 minutes at normal cruise (14 CFR 91.151): 6 more gallons, 30 in all."),
    S(1, 3, "Off-course correction", "After 60 nm you find yourself 4 nm left of course, with 80 nm to go. Using the 1-in-60 rule, how much must you turn to reach the destination directly?",
      ["7 degrees right", "4 degrees right", "3 degrees right"],
      "Drift so far is 4 nm in 60 nm, about 4 degrees. Converging on the destination needs 4 nm in 80 nm, about 3 degrees more. Turn 4 + 3 = 7 degrees right."),
    S(1, 2, "Lost procedures", "You are unsure of your position on a VFR cross-country. If weather and fuel allow, which step helps most at first?",
      ["Climb to improve radio and navaid reception and see more landmarks",
       "Descend low enough to read signs on buildings",
       "Turn off the radios and transponder to save electrical power for later"],
      "Climbing increases the range of radios, VORs and radar and widens the view. Then communicate with ATC, confess the problem and comply with instructions (AFH chapter on navigation, ACS VI.D)."),
    S(1, 2, "Victor airways", "How wide is a Victor airway within 51 nm of its navaids?",
      ["4 nm on each side of the centerline", "2 nm on each side of the centerline", "8 nm on each side of the centerline"],
      "A Victor airway includes the airspace within 4 nm of each side of its centerline, from 1,200 feet AGL up to but not including 18,000 feet MSL (AIM 1-1-3, 14 CFR 71)."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 2, "True airspeed", "Your indicated airspeed is 130 knots at 8,000 feet. Using the rule of about 2 percent per 1,000 feet, what is the approximate true airspeed?",
      ["About 151 knots", "About 130 knots", "About 140 knots"],
      "True airspeed rises about 2 percent per 1,000 feet. At 8,000 feet that is 16 percent: 130 x 1.16 = about 151 knots."),
    S(2, 2, "Time en route", "A leg is 210 nm. True airspeed is 150 knots with a 25-knot headwind. About how long is the leg?",
      ["1 hour 41 minutes", "1 hour 24 minutes", "1 hour 53 minutes"],
      "Groundspeed is 150 - 25 = 125 knots. 210 / 125 = 1.68 hours, about 1 hour 41 minutes."),
    S(2, 3, "VFR cruising altitudes", "You want to cruise VFR near 7,000 feet MSL, well above 3,000 feet AGL. Your magnetic course is 175 degrees, but a crosswind gives a magnetic heading of 185. Which altitude fits?",
      ["7,500 feet, based on the course", "8,500 feet, based on the heading", "7,000 feet, based on the course"],
      "VFR cruising altitudes above 3,000 feet AGL go by magnetic course, not heading (14 CFR 91.159). For courses from 0 to 179 degrees use odd thousands plus 500, so 7,500 feet."),
    S(2, 3, "Descent planning", "You plan a 500-foot-per-minute descent from 9,500 feet to a 1,500-foot pattern altitude at a groundspeed of 150 knots. How far out should you start down?",
      ["40 nm", "16 nm", "25 nm"],
      "The descent is 8,000 feet / 500 = 16 minutes. At 150 knots you cover 2.5 nm a minute, so 16 x 2.5 = 40 nm."),
    S(2, 2, "DME", "Flying directly over a VORTAC at 6,000 feet above it, what does the DME read?",
      ["About 1 nm", "0 nm", "About 6 nm"],
      "DME measures slant range. Directly above the station the distance is the height: 6,000 feet is about 1 nautical mile (6,076 feet)."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 3, "Wind correction", "True course is 180, true airspeed is 150 knots and the wind is from 270 at 30 knots. About what are the true heading and groundspeed?",
      ["192 degrees and 147 knots", "168 degrees and 147 knots", "192 degrees and 150 knots"],
      "The wind is directly off the right side. Wind correction angle = arcsin(30/150), about 11.5 degrees right, so heading is about 192. Groundspeed = square root of (150 squared minus 30 squared), about 147 knots."),
    S(0, 3, "Fuel planning", "You have 48 gallons usable and burn 10.5 gallons per hour. For a night VFR flight, about how long may the planned flight be?",
      ["3 hours 49 minutes", "4 hours 4 minutes", "4 hours 34 minutes"],
      "Endurance is 48 / 10.5 = 4.57 hours, about 4 hours 34 minutes. Night VFR needs a 45-minute reserve (14 CFR 91.151), leaving about 3 hours 49 minutes."),
    S(0, 2, "Great circle", "What is the shortest route between two points on the Earth?",
      ["A great circle route", "A rhumb line that keeps a constant true course", "A route following a single line of latitude"],
      "A great circle, whose plane passes through the Earth's center, is the shortest distance. Its true course changes along the way, unlike a rhumb line."),
    S(0, 2, "Flight plans", "You forget to close a VFR flight plan. When does search and rescue begin?",
      ["About 30 minutes after your estimated time of arrival", "Two hours after your estimated time of arrival, if you do not call", "Only after the destination tower reports you missing to the FAA"],
      "If a VFR flight plan is not closed within 30 minutes after the ETA, Flight Service starts search and rescue procedures (AIM 6-2-7)."),
    S(0, 2, "Radar services", "With VFR flight following from approach control, who is responsible for avoiding other traffic?",
      ["You are; the service is advisory and workload permitting", "ATC, which must separate you from all other aircraft in its airspace", "ATC, but only from IFR traffic"],
      "Basic radar service for VFR aircraft gives traffic advisories as workload allows. The pilot keeps the duty to see and avoid (AIM 4-1-15)."),
    S(0, 2, "Time zones", "You plan to arrive at 1530Z at an airport on Pacific Daylight Time, UTC minus 7. What is the local arrival time?",
      ["0830 local", "2230 local", "0730 local"],
      "Local time = UTC - 7 hours: 1530 - 0700 = 0830 Pacific Daylight Time."),
    S(0, 3, "Off-course correction", "After 50 nm you are 5 nm off course, with 100 nm to go. Using the 1-in-60 rule, how many degrees must you turn to fly directly to the destination?",
      ["9 degrees", "6 degrees", "3 degrees"],
      "Drift so far: 5 nm in 50 nm, or 6 degrees (5 / 50 x 60). Converging: 5 nm in 100 nm, or 3 degrees. Total correction is 9 degrees."),
    S(0, 2, "Compass deviation", "The compass deviation card says: For 120, steer 117. To fly a magnetic heading of 120, what compass heading do you fly?",
      ["117", "123", "120"],
      "The card lists the compass heading to steer for each magnetic heading. For 120 magnetic, steer 117 on the compass."),
    S(0, 2, "VOR", "Your OBS is set to 090, the CDI is centered and the flag shows TO. Which radial are you on?",
      ["The 270 radial", "The 090 radial", "The 180 radial"],
      "A centered needle with a TO flag on 090 means flying 090 takes you to the station, so you are west of it on the 270 radial."),
    S(0, 3, "Fuel monitoring", "You planned a 2.5-hour leg at 9 gallons per hour. After 1 hour you have used 11 gallons. At this rate, how much will the remaining 1.5 hours use?",
      ["16.5 gallons", "13.5 gallons", "11.0 gallons"],
      "The real burn is 11 gallons per hour, not 9. For the remaining 1.5 hours: 1.5 x 11 = 16.5 gallons. Recheck the reserve before continuing."),
    S(0, 2, "Diversion", "Weather forces a diversion to an alternate. What is a good first step?",
      ["Turn toward the alternate promptly on an estimated heading, then work out distance, time and fuel",
       "Circle over a landmark until you have planned the new leg in full on the chart",
       "Continue toward the original destination while you plan the whole diversion leg"],
      "The ACS (VI.C) and AFH call for turning promptly toward the alternate using a rough heading from the chart, then refining heading, distance, time and fuel once established."),
]
